SSHHEEEETT SSEETTSS
     A sheet is a file; a drawing that spans sheets is a SET of
     numbered files (_a_m_p_1._d _a_m_p_2._d, ...).  The > key saves the
     current sheet and loads the next - past the last one it of-
     fers to create it, which is how a set grows - and < walks
     back.  The Frame stamp adds ``Sheet n/m'' when the file name
     carries a number.  Shell globs are the set syntax for the
     exporters: vveelllluumm -nneett aammpp?.dd computes nets per sheet and
     MERGES them BY NAME - a net named (by an N marker or
     GND/VCC) VBUS on sheet 1 is the same conductor as VBUS on
     sheet 3; unnamed nets stay sheet-local and print qualified
     (NET-2.3 = sheet 2, net 3).  -pprriinntt emits the form feed be-
     tween sheets, and -bboomm yields ONE parts list for the whole
     set.  An off-page reference is the OFFP stencil with its
     value reading ``3'' and an N net name on its pin - the net-
     list merge makes it electrically true.

     A BUS is an idiom, not an object: draw the trunk as a BOLD
     wire - graphics, not electricity - and tap it with short
     wires carrying NN DD00 ...  NN DD77; same-named taps merge across
     the whole sheet set, which is what a bus MEANS.  -cchheecckk
     flags a net name appearing on fewer than two pins in the set
     - the typo'd bit (D3 on one end, D8 on the other) that eyes
     cannot see.

